---
title: Resumable Streams (Advanced)
id: advanced
description: "The durability contract, terminal and error handling, reconnect tuning, joinRun, Cloudflare deployment, and process-death recovery for resumable streams."
keywords:
  - stream durability
  - durableStream options
  - joinRun
  - StreamReconnectLimitError
  - DurableStreamIncompleteError
  - process death
  - cloudflare durable streams
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"./overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers the common case: pick an adapter, wrap your\nresponse, add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler. This page covers the rest."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"durablestream-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"durableStream options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream(request, options)"},{"type":"text","value":" talks to an external\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://durablestreams.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" backend:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your token source.\nimport { getDurableStreamsToken } from './auth'\n\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  headers: async () => ({\n    Authorization: `Bearer ${await getDurableStreamsToken()}`,\n  }),\n}\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: {\n      adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      batch: 32,\n      batchWaitMs: 50,\n    },\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":" takes a static object for fixed credentials or an async resolver for\nrotating tokens. The resolver runs for every create, append, read, and close."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The backend must return a non-empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Stream-Next-Offset"},{"type":"text","value":" header on create,\nappend, and close. A missing header fails loudly. The adapter never guesses an\noffset."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":". Its offsets embed a\nbackend-assigned cursor, so a caller cannot choose one, and the adapter has no\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":" to re-persist a range at offsets it did not mint. Code that requires\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UpsertableStreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" fails to compile at the wiring site rather than at\nrun time. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Re-persisting a stored range"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor adapters that can offer it. Resuming a run after a producer restart does\nnot need it: see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"batch-the-log-writes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch the log writes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every append to a remote backend is one request. A chunk reaches the client only\nafter its append. So two options on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" trade writes against how fast\nthe text appears:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"batch"},{"type":"text","value":": the most chunks in one append. The default is 32."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"batchWaitMs"},{"type":"text","value":": the most milliseconds that a chunk waits for more chunks. Then\nits batch is appended and sent. The default is 50."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your backend charges per write, raise "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"batchWaitMs"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"batch"},{"type":"text","value":". The text then\narrives in bigger steps. If the reply arrives in large blocks, lower\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"batchWaitMs"},{"type":"text","value":". Set it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" to append every chunk on its own."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"attaching-to-a-run-by-id","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Attaching to a run by id"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnect after a drop is automatic. To attach to a run from the start on\npurpose (a second tab, or a full reload where you already know the run id), call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":". It performs a read-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset=-1"},{"type":"text","value":", which is why the\nserver needs the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler from the Overview. That handler is just\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter })"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeHttpResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" for\nNDJSON): it replays from the log and returns a 400 if the request has no resume\noffset."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nasync function attach(runId: string) {\n  const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n  for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId)) {\n    console.log(chunk)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All four HTTP adapters ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":") expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"disconnect-stop-and-errors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disconnect, stop, and errors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A durable run's producer is decoupled from the delivery socket. When the client\ndisconnects (a page reload, a dropped connection), the response is cancelled but\nthe run keeps draining into the log to its own terminal, so a reconnect or a\nmount-time "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" tails it to completion. This is what makes a run resumable\nacross a full reload, not just an in-session reconnect. If that replay ends on\na client tool, the client sends the tool result after the replay finishes. That\nis the same path as a live stream."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A run ends early only on a genuine cancel or a failure:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortController"},{"type":"text","value":" you pass to the response as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abortController"},{"type":"text","value":" and then abort (from a user Stop button, or by forwarding\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.signal"},{"type":"text","value":", which is an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":", onto a controller of your own).\nThis stops the producer and appends a terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":". A bare client\ndisconnect does not do this; pass a controller when you want a disconnect to\nalso stop the run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider failure"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — the model stream throws; the error is appended as a\nterminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Either way the producer awaits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" on exit, and a terminal is written\nbefore closing so a reconnecting or joining client sees a terminal instead of\nhanging. If appending that terminal or closing fails, the cause is logged\nserver-side by default (a joiner only ever sees a generic incomplete error, so\nthe server log is where the real cause lives). The server logs a failed replay\nthe same way, for example a reconnect or join for an expired run. On SSE and\nNDJSON, that reader also gets a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"debug"},{"type":"text","value":" to route these logs\nto your own logger:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { memoryStream, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction respond(request: Request, stream: AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>) {\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    debug: true, // or { logger } for a custom Logger\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A durable source must end with its own terminal event\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":"). On normal completion the log is terminalized only\nif the source emitted a terminal; without one, a durable consumer reconnects\nonce, makes no progress, and fails with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableStreamIncompleteError"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nalways emits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":", so this only affects hand-rolled streams."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"memorystream-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"memoryStream in production"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Within a single live process "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" already survives a client\ndisconnect: the producer outlives the socket and keeps draining into the log, so\na later reconnect or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" resumes a still-running run. Two things still keep\nit to development and single-process deployments:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The log lives in one process's memory, so a reconnect that lands on a\ndifferent worker finds nothing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the process itself dies, the log dies with it, so an interrupted run can\nneither resume nor terminalize on its own. A production backend adds a\nlease/reaper (see "},{"type":"link","href":"#process-death","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process death"}]},{"type":"text","value":") that an in-memory process\ncannot."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Completed runs are evicted after a grace window, so resuming an expired or\nunknown run fails loudly instead of hanging, and a from-start join to a run that\nnever produces fails after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstChunkDeadlineMs"},{"type":"text","value":". Spanning processes (point 1)\nis what "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" adds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reconnection-bounding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnection bounding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dropped connection resumes from the last offset. A transport error retries as\nlong as an offset is held, even if that attempt delivered only the replayed\noverlap. A durable run that ends cleanly without a terminal and makes no forward\nprogress fails with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableStreamIncompleteError"},{"type":"text","value":". Only a non-durable (untagged)\nstream that ends cleanly counts as a completed run. The distinction is\ndeliberate: a clean close means the server ended the response, so a durable\ntransport must never end an empty long-poll window while the producer is alive."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client throttles between attempts and bounds reconnection with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAttempts"},{"type":"text","value":",\nfailing with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamReconnectLimitError"},{"type":"text","value":". The ceiling counts only consecutive\nreconnects that deliver no new events; forward progress resets it to zero. A\nhealthy long run, even one behind a proxy that rolls the socket after every\nevent, never approaches it. It fires only when a run is genuinely stuck."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nfunction makeConnection() {\n  return fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat', {\n    reconnect: { maxAttempts: 5, delayMs: 250 }, // defaults shown\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds its own read loop the same way. After a mid-window body\nread failure it retries from the last valid position, capping consecutive\nfailures ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconnect: { maxReadFailures: 10, delayMs: 250 }"},{"type":"text","value":"). Normal long-poll\nadvancement is never throttled."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"websocket-transport","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebSocket transport"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Everything above describes SSE and NDJSON: one connection per turn. A\nWebSocket instead stays open for the whole conversation, so a few things work\ndifferently. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./websockets","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebSockets"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full protocol and code;\nthis section covers only what changes relative to the rest of this page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnect rides in the URL, not a header."}]},{"type":"text","value":" SSE and NDJSON resume with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":" because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":"/XHR can set arbitrary headers before opening a\nrequest. A browser's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":" constructor can't set custom headers on the\nhandshake, so the offset instead rides in the URL: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" reopens at that URL automatically on a durable run's drop, the\nsame de-dupe guarantee as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":", just carried differently on\nthe wire."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The socket is conversation-scoped, not run-scoped."}]},{"type":"text","value":" One socket carries many\nturns. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" option is therefore a factory keyed\nby each turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" (via a synthetic per-turn request), not a single value\nbuilt once. The socket itself closes on client close, an idle timeout, or\nprocess shutdown, not when one turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" arrives."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heartbeat and idle timeout replace the transport-level keepalive SSE gets\nfor free."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" pings every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"heartbeatMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 30s) and\ncloses the socket after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idleTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 5 minutes) with no inbound\nframe — but never while a turn is still streaming, so a long single\ngeneration is safe."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An abort frame targets one turn, not the socket."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ type: 'abort', runId }"},{"type":"text","value":"\naborts only that turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRun"},{"type":"text","value":" iteration; the socket stays open for the next\nturn. Closing the socket aborts every turn still in flight on it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hosting differs by runtime."}]},{"type":"text","value":" On Cloudflare Workers or Durable Objects,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketPair"},{"type":"text","value":" and needs no manual\nupgrade. On Node (or anywhere without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketPair"},{"type":"text","value":"), you upgrade the\nconnection yourself, typically with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ws"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })"},{"type":"text","value":"\nhooked into the HTTP server's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upgrade"},{"type":"text","value":" event, and hand the resulting socket to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" directly."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The producer-vs-socket caveat from "},{"type":"link","href":"#memorystream-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"memoryStream in\nproduction"}]},{"type":"text","value":" applies unchanged."}]},{"type":"text","value":" With\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":", a dropped WebSocket aborts the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call backing it just\nlike a dropped SSE connection does; reconnecting replays the log rather than\nresuming a still-running model call. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" decouples the two exactly\nas it does for SSE and NDJSON."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"offset-ownership","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offset ownership"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability<TOffset>"},{"type":"text","value":" owns its offset format. Core only passes returned\nvalues back to that adapter and writes them to the wire (an SSE "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id:"},{"type":"text","value":" field, or\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" of an NDJSON "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ id, chunk }"},{"type":"text","value":" envelope). For every appended batch:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"core calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append(chunks)"},{"type":"text","value":" before forwarding the chunks;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the adapter returns exactly one offset per chunk, in order;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"core rejects missing, extra, empty, or whitespace/CR/LF-containing offsets;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a resume reads strictly after the supplied offset."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core never derives an offset from an array index and never stamps one onto the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamChunk"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A producer that restarts mid-run and replays part of its source must not append\nthe overlap twice. The client has no safety net below its offset de-dup: a\nre-appended chunk carries a new offset, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seen"},{"type":"text","value":" never suppresses it, and the\nstream processor concatenates text deltas and tool-call arguments\nunconditionally. That is duplicated prose and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}"},{"type":"text","value":" arguments, not a\ndegraded experience."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mechanism for it is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" plus plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append"},{"type":"text","value":", not caller-chosen\noffsets. Read what the log already holds, compare it against your replay,\nsuppress the matching prefix, and append only the remainder:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { memoryStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nasync function appendAfterStored(\n  request: Request,\n  replayed: Array<StreamChunk>,\n) {\n  const durability = memoryStream(request)\n  const stored = await durability.snapshot()\n  // `snapshot` returns without waiting, even while the log is open, so this\n  // works on a log whose previous producer died without calling `close()`.\n  const remainder = replayed.slice(stored.length)\n  if (remainder.length > 0) await durability.append(remainder)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is what "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" does for a replayed agent run, comparing by\na timestamp-insensitive fingerprint rather than by count, and throwing if the\nreplay and the log disagree inside the prefix. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that path in full."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the log stays append-only with adapter-minted offsets, this works on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" too, which is the point: it is the recommended production\nadapter and it has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":". Two caveats on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream.snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nspecifically. It is bounded by an internal ceiling on how many windows it will\npull before giving up on a backend that never reports the reader caught up, and\nit reads from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"},{"type":"text","value":", so it cannot answer "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":" for a stream the backend never\ncreated; that is a failed call, not an empty run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":" remains available as an optional capability for adapters whose store\ncan write at a caller-chosen key. Nothing in the resume path above uses it, and\nmost integrations never ask for it. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" offers it, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndoes not (see above). See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Re-persisting a stored range"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to\nbuild your own."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cloudflare-durable-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Durable Streams"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://durablestreams.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ships a Cloudflare Workers plus\nDurable Objects backend that speaks this protocol, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" talks to\nit directly with no new adapter."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When your TanStack AI endpoint also runs on Workers, reach the backend over a\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"service binding"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ninstead of a public URL. The adapter's injectable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" routes every request\nthrough the binding, so traffic stays on Cloudflare's network and the binding\n(not a bearer token) authorizes the call:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\ninterface Env {\n  // A service binding to the deployed Durable Streams Worker.\n  DURABLE_STREAMS: { fetch: typeof fetch }\n}\n\nfunction cloudflareAdapter(request: Request, env: Env) {\n  // No `server` needed: the binding routes by path, so the adapter uses an\n  // internal placeholder base and only the `/streams/...` path matters.\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n    fetch: env.DURABLE_STREAMS.fetch.bind(env.DURABLE_STREAMS),\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the backend runs elsewhere, point "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" at the Worker's public URL\ninstead:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\nfunction urlAdapter(request: Request) {\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    server: 'https://durable-streams.example.workers.dev',\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Running on a Durable Object also satisfies the lease/reaper described under\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#process-death","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process death"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a DO alarm can terminalize a run whose producer\ndied, so a reconnecting client sees a terminal state instead of waiting forever."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"process-death","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process death"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A process that has already terminated cannot run cleanup code, so literal\nprocess death cannot be guaranteed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" alone. Production\nbackends should add a lease/reaper:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the producer acquires or renews a lease while writing;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a timer, alarm, or background worker detects expired leases;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the reaper records an aborted terminal state and closes the log;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"readers observe that terminal state instead of waiting forever."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This belongs to the durability service or deployment, not the in-process\nresponse helper."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One case has a mechanism rather than only advice. A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sandboxed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" run's work\nlives on inside the sandbox rather than in the dead process, so a later request\ncan adopt it: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxRunDriver"},{"type":"text","value":" claims the run, fences out the host that died,\nand resumes appending to the same log. That is a takeover of a live producer, not\nterminalization of a dead one, and it only applies to sandboxed runs — see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The advice above still stands for\neverything else. Note also that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot participate: its log lives\nin the producer's own process, so nothing survives that process to be adopted."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"delivery-is-not-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delivery is not state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The durability log replays chunks. It is not a queryable source of truth for\nthread messages or conversation history. It answers \"what did this run stream?\",\nnot \"what has this user said?\". Keep authoritative state in your own storage.\nSee "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/client-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the client-side options."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"durablestream-options","text":"durableStream options","level":2},{"id":"batch-the-log-writes","text":"Batch the log writes","level":3},{"id":"attaching-to-a-run-by-id","text":"Attaching to a run by id","level":2},{"id":"disconnect-stop-and-errors","text":"Disconnect, stop, and errors","level":2},{"id":"memorystream-in-production","text":"memoryStream in production","level":2},{"id":"reconnection-bounding","text":"Reconnection bounding","level":2},{"id":"websocket-transport","text":"WebSocket transport","level":2},{"id":"offset-ownership","text":"Offset ownership","level":2},{"id":"resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed","text":"Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed","level":3},{"id":"cloudflare-durable-streams","text":"Cloudflare Durable Streams","level":2},{"id":"process-death","text":"Process death","level":2},{"id":"delivery-is-not-state","text":"Delivery is not state","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe [Overview](./overview) covers the common case: pick an adapter, wrap your\nresponse, add a `GET` handler. This page covers the rest.\n\n## durableStream options\n\n`durableStream(request, options)` talks to an external\n[Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) backend:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your token source.\nimport { getDurableStreamsToken } from './auth'\n\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  headers: async () => ({\n    Authorization: `Bearer ${await getDurableStreamsToken()}`,\n  }),\n}\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: {\n      adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      batch: 32,\n      batchWaitMs: 50,\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\n- `headers` takes a static object for fixed credentials or an async resolver for\n  rotating tokens. The resolver runs for every create, append, read, and close.\n- The backend must return a non-empty `Stream-Next-Offset` header on create,\n  append, and close. A missing header fails loudly. The adapter never guesses an\n  offset.\n- `durableStream` returns a plain `StreamDurability`. Its offsets embed a\n  backend-assigned cursor, so a caller cannot choose one, and the adapter has no\n  `upsert` to re-persist a range at offsets it did not mint. Code that requires\n  `UpsertableStreamDurability` fails to compile at the wiring site rather than at\n  run time. See\n  [Re-persisting a stored range](./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range)\n  for adapters that can offer it. Resuming a run after a producer restart does\n  not need it: see\n  [Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed](#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed).\n\n### Batch the log writes\n\nEvery append to a remote backend is one request. A chunk reaches the client only\nafter its append. So two options on `durability` trade writes against how fast\nthe text appears:\n\n- `batch`: the most chunks in one append. The default is 32.\n- `batchWaitMs`: the most milliseconds that a chunk waits for more chunks. Then\n  its batch is appended and sent. The default is 50.\n\nIf your backend charges per write, raise `batchWaitMs` and `batch`. The text then\narrives in bigger steps. If the reply arrives in large blocks, lower\n`batchWaitMs`. Set it to `0` to append every chunk on its own.\n\n## Attaching to a run by id\n\nReconnect after a drop is automatic. To attach to a run from the start on\npurpose (a second tab, or a full reload where you already know the run id), call\n`joinRun`. It performs a read-only `GET` with `offset=-1`, which is why the\nserver needs the `GET` handler from the Overview. That handler is just\n`resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter })` (or `resumeHttpResponse` for\nNDJSON): it replays from the log and returns a 400 if the request has no resume\noffset.\n\n```ts\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nasync function attach(runId: string) {\n  const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n  for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId)) {\n    console.log(chunk)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll four HTTP adapters (`fetchServerSentEvents`, `fetchHttpStream`,\n`xhrServerSentEvents`, `xhrHttpStream`) expose `joinRun`.\n\n## Disconnect, stop, and errors\n\nA durable run's producer is decoupled from the delivery socket. When the client\ndisconnects (a page reload, a dropped connection), the response is cancelled but\nthe run keeps draining into the log to its own terminal, so a reconnect or a\nmount-time `joinRun` tails it to completion. This is what makes a run resumable\nacross a full reload, not just an in-session reconnect. If that replay ends on\na client tool, the client sends the tool result after the replay finishes. That\nis the same path as a live stream.\n\nA run ends early only on a genuine cancel or a failure:\n\n- **Cancel** — an `AbortController` you pass to the response as\n  `abortController` and then abort (from a user Stop button, or by forwarding\n  `request.signal`, which is an `AbortSignal`, onto a controller of your own).\n  This stops the producer and appends a terminal `RUN_ERROR`. A bare client\n  disconnect does not do this; pass a controller when you want a disconnect to\n  also stop the run.\n- **Provider failure** — the model stream throws; the error is appended as a\n  terminal `RUN_ERROR`.\n\nEither way the producer awaits `close()` on exit, and a terminal is written\nbefore closing so a reconnecting or joining client sees a terminal instead of\nhanging. If appending that terminal or closing fails, the cause is logged\nserver-side by default (a joiner only ever sees a generic incomplete error, so\nthe server log is where the real cause lives). The server logs a failed replay\nthe same way, for example a reconnect or join for an expired run. On SSE and\nNDJSON, that reader also gets a `RUN_ERROR`. Pass `debug` to route these logs\nto your own logger:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction respond(request: Request, stream: AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>) {\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    debug: true, // or { logger } for a custom Logger\n  })\n}\n```\n\nA durable source must end with its own terminal event\n(`RUN_FINISHED`/`RUN_ERROR`). On normal completion the log is terminalized only\nif the source emitted a terminal; without one, a durable consumer reconnects\nonce, makes no progress, and fails with `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. `chat()`\nalways emits `RUN_FINISHED`, so this only affects hand-rolled streams.\n\n## memoryStream in production\n\nWithin a single live process `memoryStream` already survives a client\ndisconnect: the producer outlives the socket and keeps draining into the log, so\na later reconnect or `joinRun` resumes a still-running run. Two things still keep\nit to development and single-process deployments:\n\n1. The log lives in one process's memory, so a reconnect that lands on a\n   different worker finds nothing.\n2. If the process itself dies, the log dies with it, so an interrupted run can\n   neither resume nor terminalize on its own. A production backend adds a\n   lease/reaper (see [Process death](#process-death)) that an in-memory process\n   cannot.\n\nCompleted runs are evicted after a grace window, so resuming an expired or\nunknown run fails loudly instead of hanging, and a from-start join to a run that\nnever produces fails after `firstChunkDeadlineMs`. Spanning processes (point 1)\nis what `durableStream` adds.\n\n## Reconnection bounding\n\nA dropped connection resumes from the last offset. A transport error retries as\nlong as an offset is held, even if that attempt delivered only the replayed\noverlap. A durable run that ends cleanly without a terminal and makes no forward\nprogress fails with `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. Only a non-durable (untagged)\nstream that ends cleanly counts as a completed run. The distinction is\ndeliberate: a clean close means the server ended the response, so a durable\ntransport must never end an empty long-poll window while the producer is alive.\n\nThe client throttles between attempts and bounds reconnection with `maxAttempts`,\nfailing with `StreamReconnectLimitError`. The ceiling counts only consecutive\nreconnects that deliver no new events; forward progress resets it to zero. A\nhealthy long run, even one behind a proxy that rolls the socket after every\nevent, never approaches it. It fires only when a run is genuinely stuck.\n\n```ts\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nfunction makeConnection() {\n  return fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat', {\n    reconnect: { maxAttempts: 5, delayMs: 250 }, // defaults shown\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`durableStream` bounds its own read loop the same way. After a mid-window body\nread failure it retries from the last valid position, capping consecutive\nfailures (`reconnect: { maxReadFailures: 10, delayMs: 250 }`). Normal long-poll\nadvancement is never throttled.\n\n## WebSocket transport\n\nEverything above describes SSE and NDJSON: one connection per turn. A\nWebSocket instead stays open for the whole conversation, so a few things work\ndifferently. See [WebSockets](./websockets) for the full protocol and code;\nthis section covers only what changes relative to the rest of this page.\n\n**Reconnect rides in the URL, not a header.** SSE and NDJSON resume with\n`Last-Event-ID` because `fetch`/XHR can set arbitrary headers before opening a\nrequest. A browser's `WebSocket` constructor can't set custom headers on the\nhandshake, so the offset instead rides in the URL: `?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>`.\n`webSocket()` reopens at that URL automatically on a durable run's drop, the\nsame de-dupe guarantee as `fetchServerSentEvents`, just carried differently on\nthe wire.\n\n**The socket is conversation-scoped, not run-scoped.** One socket carries many\nturns. `toWebSocketStream`'s `durability` option is therefore a factory keyed\nby each turn's `runId` (via a synthetic per-turn request), not a single value\nbuilt once. The socket itself closes on client close, an idle timeout, or\nprocess shutdown, not when one turn's `RUN_FINISHED` arrives.\n\n**Heartbeat and idle timeout replace the transport-level keepalive SSE gets\nfor free.** `toWebSocketStream` pings every `heartbeatMs` (default 30s) and\ncloses the socket after `idleTimeoutMs` (default 5 minutes) with no inbound\nframe — but never while a turn is still streaming, so a long single\ngeneration is safe.\n\n**An abort frame targets one turn, not the socket.** `{ type: 'abort', runId }`\naborts only that turn's `onRun` iteration; the socket stays open for the next\nturn. Closing the socket aborts every turn still in flight on it.\n\n**Hosting differs by runtime.** On Cloudflare Workers or Durable Objects,\n`toWebSocketResponse` uses the global `WebSocketPair` and needs no manual\nupgrade. On Node (or anywhere without `WebSocketPair`), you upgrade the\nconnection yourself, typically with `ws`'s `WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })`\nhooked into the HTTP server's `upgrade` event, and hand the resulting socket to\n`toWebSocketStream` / `resumeWebSocketStream` directly.\n\n**The producer-vs-socket caveat from [memoryStream in\nproduction](#memorystream-in-production) applies unchanged.** With\n`memoryStream`, a dropped WebSocket aborts the `chat()` call backing it just\nlike a dropped SSE connection does; reconnecting replays the log rather than\nresuming a still-running model call. `durableStream` decouples the two exactly\nas it does for SSE and NDJSON.\n\n## Offset ownership\n\n`StreamDurability<TOffset>` owns its offset format. Core only passes returned\nvalues back to that adapter and writes them to the wire (an SSE `id:` field, or\nthe `id` of an NDJSON `{ id, chunk }` envelope). For every appended batch:\n\n1. core calls `append(chunks)` before forwarding the chunks;\n2. the adapter returns exactly one offset per chunk, in order;\n3. core rejects missing, extra, empty, or whitespace/CR/LF-containing offsets;\n4. a resume reads strictly after the supplied offset.\n\nCore never derives an offset from an array index and never stamps one onto the\n`StreamChunk`.\n\n### Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed\n\nA producer that restarts mid-run and replays part of its source must not append\nthe overlap twice. The client has no safety net below its offset de-dup: a\nre-appended chunk carries a new offset, so `seen` never suppresses it, and the\nstream processor concatenates text deltas and tool-call arguments\nunconditionally. That is duplicated prose and `{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}` arguments, not a\ndegraded experience.\n\nThe mechanism for it is `snapshot()` plus plain `append`, not caller-chosen\noffsets. Read what the log already holds, compare it against your replay,\nsuppress the matching prefix, and append only the remainder:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nasync function appendAfterStored(\n  request: Request,\n  replayed: Array<StreamChunk>,\n) {\n  const durability = memoryStream(request)\n  const stored = await durability.snapshot()\n  // `snapshot` returns without waiting, even while the log is open, so this\n  // works on a log whose previous producer died without calling `close()`.\n  const remainder = replayed.slice(stored.length)\n  if (remainder.length > 0) await durability.append(remainder)\n}\n```\n\nThis is what `@tanstack/ai-sandbox` does for a replayed agent run, comparing by\na timestamp-insensitive fingerprint rather than by count, and throwing if the\nreplay and the log disagree inside the prefix. See\n[The Run Journal](../sandbox/journal) for that path in full.\n\nBecause the log stays append-only with adapter-minted offsets, this works on\n`durableStream` too, which is the point: it is the recommended production\nadapter and it has no `upsert`. Two caveats on `durableStream.snapshot()`\nspecifically. It is bounded by an internal ceiling on how many windows it will\npull before giving up on a backend that never reports the reader caught up, and\nit reads from `-1`, so it cannot answer `[]` for a stream the backend never\ncreated; that is a failed call, not an empty run.\n\n`upsert` remains available as an optional capability for adapters whose store\ncan write at a caller-chosen key. Nothing in the resume path above uses it, and\nmost integrations never ask for it. `memoryStream` offers it, `durableStream`\ndoes not (see above). See\n[Re-persisting a stored range](./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range) to\nbuild your own.\n\n## Cloudflare Durable Streams\n\n[Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) ships a Cloudflare Workers plus\nDurable Objects backend that speaks this protocol, so `durableStream` talks to\nit directly with no new adapter.\n\nWhen your TanStack AI endpoint also runs on Workers, reach the backend over a\n[service binding](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/)\ninstead of a public URL. The adapter's injectable `fetch` routes every request\nthrough the binding, so traffic stays on Cloudflare's network and the binding\n(not a bearer token) authorizes the call:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\ninterface Env {\n  // A service binding to the deployed Durable Streams Worker.\n  DURABLE_STREAMS: { fetch: typeof fetch }\n}\n\nfunction cloudflareAdapter(request: Request, env: Env) {\n  // No `server` needed: the binding routes by path, so the adapter uses an\n  // internal placeholder base and only the `/streams/...` path matters.\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n    fetch: env.DURABLE_STREAMS.fetch.bind(env.DURABLE_STREAMS),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nIf the backend runs elsewhere, point `server` at the Worker's public URL\ninstead:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\nfunction urlAdapter(request: Request) {\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    server: 'https://durable-streams.example.workers.dev',\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  })\n}\n```\n\nRunning on a Durable Object also satisfies the lease/reaper described under\n[Process death](#process-death): a DO alarm can terminalize a run whose producer\ndied, so a reconnecting client sees a terminal state instead of waiting forever.\n\n## Process death\n\nA process that has already terminated cannot run cleanup code, so literal\nprocess death cannot be guaranteed by `finally` or `close()` alone. Production\nbackends should add a lease/reaper:\n\n1. the producer acquires or renews a lease while writing;\n2. a timer, alarm, or background worker detects expired leases;\n3. the reaper records an aborted terminal state and closes the log;\n4. readers observe that terminal state instead of waiting forever.\n\nThis belongs to the durability service or deployment, not the in-process\nresponse helper.\n\nOne case has a mechanism rather than only advice. A **sandboxed** run's work\nlives on inside the sandbox rather than in the dead process, so a later request\ncan adopt it: `sandboxRunDriver` claims the run, fences out the host that died,\nand resumes appending to the same log. That is a takeover of a live producer, not\nterminalization of a dead one, and it only applies to sandboxed runs — see\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover). The advice above still stands for\neverything else. Note also that `memoryStream` cannot participate: its log lives\nin the producer's own process, so nothing survives that process to be adopted.\n\n## Delivery is not state\n\nThe durability log replays chunks. It is not a queryable source of truth for\nthread messages or conversation history. It answers \"what did this run stream?\",\nnot \"what has this user said?\". Keep authoritative state in your own storage.\nSee [Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence) for the client-side options.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Resumable Streams (Advanced)\nid: advanced\ndescription: \"The durability contract, terminal and error handling, reconnect tuning, joinRun, Cloudflare deployment, and process-death recovery for resumable streams.\"\nkeywords:\n  - stream durability\n  - durableStream options\n  - joinRun\n  - StreamReconnectLimitError\n  - DurableStreamIncompleteError\n  - process death\n  - cloudflare durable streams\n---\n\nThe [Overview](./overview) covers the common case: pick an adapter, wrap your\nresponse, add a `GET` handler. This page covers the rest.\n\n## durableStream options\n\n`durableStream(request, options)` talks to an external\n[Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) backend:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your token source.\nimport { getDurableStreamsToken } from './auth'\n\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  headers: async () => ({\n    Authorization: `Bearer ${await getDurableStreamsToken()}`,\n  }),\n}\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: {\n      adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      batch: 32,\n      batchWaitMs: 50,\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\n- `headers` takes a static object for fixed credentials or an async resolver for\n  rotating tokens. The resolver runs for every create, append, read, and close.\n- The backend must return a non-empty `Stream-Next-Offset` header on create,\n  append, and close. A missing header fails loudly. The adapter never guesses an\n  offset.\n- `durableStream` returns a plain `StreamDurability`. Its offsets embed a\n  backend-assigned cursor, so a caller cannot choose one, and the adapter has no\n  `upsert` to re-persist a range at offsets it did not mint. Code that requires\n  `UpsertableStreamDurability` fails to compile at the wiring site rather than at\n  run time. See\n  [Re-persisting a stored range](./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range)\n  for adapters that can offer it. Resuming a run after a producer restart does\n  not need it: see\n  [Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed](#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed).\n\n### Batch the log writes\n\nEvery append to a remote backend is one request. A chunk reaches the client only\nafter its append. So two options on `durability` trade writes against how fast\nthe text appears:\n\n- `batch`: the most chunks in one append. The default is 32.\n- `batchWaitMs`: the most milliseconds that a chunk waits for more chunks. Then\n  its batch is appended and sent. The default is 50.\n\nIf your backend charges per write, raise `batchWaitMs` and `batch`. The text then\narrives in bigger steps. If the reply arrives in large blocks, lower\n`batchWaitMs`. Set it to `0` to append every chunk on its own.\n\n## Attaching to a run by id\n\nReconnect after a drop is automatic. To attach to a run from the start on\npurpose (a second tab, or a full reload where you already know the run id), call\n`joinRun`. It performs a read-only `GET` with `offset=-1`, which is why the\nserver needs the `GET` handler from the Overview. That handler is just\n`resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter })` (or `resumeHttpResponse` for\nNDJSON): it replays from the log and returns a 400 if the request has no resume\noffset.\n\n```ts\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nasync function attach(runId: string) {\n  const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n  for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId)) {\n    console.log(chunk)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll four HTTP adapters (`fetchServerSentEvents`, `fetchHttpStream`,\n`xhrServerSentEvents`, `xhrHttpStream`) expose `joinRun`.\n\n## Disconnect, stop, and errors\n\nA durable run's producer is decoupled from the delivery socket. When the client\ndisconnects (a page reload, a dropped connection), the response is cancelled but\nthe run keeps draining into the log to its own terminal, so a reconnect or a\nmount-time `joinRun` tails it to completion. This is what makes a run resumable\nacross a full reload, not just an in-session reconnect. If that replay ends on\na client tool, the client sends the tool result after the replay finishes. That\nis the same path as a live stream.\n\nA run ends early only on a genuine cancel or a failure:\n\n- **Cancel** — an `AbortController` you pass to the response as\n  `abortController` and then abort (from a user Stop button, or by forwarding\n  `request.signal`, which is an `AbortSignal`, onto a controller of your own).\n  This stops the producer and appends a terminal `RUN_ERROR`. A bare client\n  disconnect does not do this; pass a controller when you want a disconnect to\n  also stop the run.\n- **Provider failure** — the model stream throws; the error is appended as a\n  terminal `RUN_ERROR`.\n\nEither way the producer awaits `close()` on exit, and a terminal is written\nbefore closing so a reconnecting or joining client sees a terminal instead of\nhanging. If appending that terminal or closing fails, the cause is logged\nserver-side by default (a joiner only ever sees a generic incomplete error, so\nthe server log is where the real cause lives). The server logs a failed replay\nthe same way, for example a reconnect or join for an expired run. On SSE and\nNDJSON, that reader also gets a `RUN_ERROR`. Pass `debug` to route these logs\nto your own logger:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction respond(request: Request, stream: AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>) {\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    debug: true, // or { logger } for a custom Logger\n  })\n}\n```\n\nA durable source must end with its own terminal event\n(`RUN_FINISHED`/`RUN_ERROR`). On normal completion the log is terminalized only\nif the source emitted a terminal; without one, a durable consumer reconnects\nonce, makes no progress, and fails with `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. `chat()`\nalways emits `RUN_FINISHED`, so this only affects hand-rolled streams.\n\n## memoryStream in production\n\nWithin a single live process `memoryStream` already survives a client\ndisconnect: the producer outlives the socket and keeps draining into the log, so\na later reconnect or `joinRun` resumes a still-running run. Two things still keep\nit to development and single-process deployments:\n\n1. The log lives in one process's memory, so a reconnect that lands on a\n   different worker finds nothing.\n2. If the process itself dies, the log dies with it, so an interrupted run can\n   neither resume nor terminalize on its own. A production backend adds a\n   lease/reaper (see [Process death](#process-death)) that an in-memory process\n   cannot.\n\nCompleted runs are evicted after a grace window, so resuming an expired or\nunknown run fails loudly instead of hanging, and a from-start join to a run that\nnever produces fails after `firstChunkDeadlineMs`. Spanning processes (point 1)\nis what `durableStream` adds.\n\n## Reconnection bounding\n\nA dropped connection resumes from the last offset. A transport error retries as\nlong as an offset is held, even if that attempt delivered only the replayed\noverlap. A durable run that ends cleanly without a terminal and makes no forward\nprogress fails with `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. Only a non-durable (untagged)\nstream that ends cleanly counts as a completed run. The distinction is\ndeliberate: a clean close means the server ended the response, so a durable\ntransport must never end an empty long-poll window while the producer is alive.\n\nThe client throttles between attempts and bounds reconnection with `maxAttempts`,\nfailing with `StreamReconnectLimitError`. The ceiling counts only consecutive\nreconnects that deliver no new events; forward progress resets it to zero. A\nhealthy long run, even one behind a proxy that rolls the socket after every\nevent, never approaches it. It fires only when a run is genuinely stuck.\n\n```ts\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nfunction makeConnection() {\n  return fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat', {\n    reconnect: { maxAttempts: 5, delayMs: 250 }, // defaults shown\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`durableStream` bounds its own read loop the same way. After a mid-window body\nread failure it retries from the last valid position, capping consecutive\nfailures (`reconnect: { maxReadFailures: 10, delayMs: 250 }`). Normal long-poll\nadvancement is never throttled.\n\n## WebSocket transport\n\nEverything above describes SSE and NDJSON: one connection per turn. A\nWebSocket instead stays open for the whole conversation, so a few things work\ndifferently. See [WebSockets](./websockets) for the full protocol and code;\nthis section covers only what changes relative to the rest of this page.\n\n**Reconnect rides in the URL, not a header.** SSE and NDJSON resume with\n`Last-Event-ID` because `fetch`/XHR can set arbitrary headers before opening a\nrequest. A browser's `WebSocket` constructor can't set custom headers on the\nhandshake, so the offset instead rides in the URL: `?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>`.\n`webSocket()` reopens at that URL automatically on a durable run's drop, the\nsame de-dupe guarantee as `fetchServerSentEvents`, just carried differently on\nthe wire.\n\n**The socket is conversation-scoped, not run-scoped.** One socket carries many\nturns. `toWebSocketStream`'s `durability` option is therefore a factory keyed\nby each turn's `runId` (via a synthetic per-turn request), not a single value\nbuilt once. The socket itself closes on client close, an idle timeout, or\nprocess shutdown, not when one turn's `RUN_FINISHED` arrives.\n\n**Heartbeat and idle timeout replace the transport-level keepalive SSE gets\nfor free.** `toWebSocketStream` pings every `heartbeatMs` (default 30s) and\ncloses the socket after `idleTimeoutMs` (default 5 minutes) with no inbound\nframe — but never while a turn is still streaming, so a long single\ngeneration is safe.\n\n**An abort frame targets one turn, not the socket.** `{ type: 'abort', runId }`\naborts only that turn's `onRun` iteration; the socket stays open for the next\nturn. Closing the socket aborts every turn still in flight on it.\n\n**Hosting differs by runtime.** On Cloudflare Workers or Durable Objects,\n`toWebSocketResponse` uses the global `WebSocketPair` and needs no manual\nupgrade. On Node (or anywhere without `WebSocketPair`), you upgrade the\nconnection yourself, typically with `ws`'s `WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })`\nhooked into the HTTP server's `upgrade` event, and hand the resulting socket to\n`toWebSocketStream` / `resumeWebSocketStream` directly.\n\n**The producer-vs-socket caveat from [memoryStream in\nproduction](#memorystream-in-production) applies unchanged.** With\n`memoryStream`, a dropped WebSocket aborts the `chat()` call backing it just\nlike a dropped SSE connection does; reconnecting replays the log rather than\nresuming a still-running model call. `durableStream` decouples the two exactly\nas it does for SSE and NDJSON.\n\n## Offset ownership\n\n`StreamDurability<TOffset>` owns its offset format. Core only passes returned\nvalues back to that adapter and writes them to the wire (an SSE `id:` field, or\nthe `id` of an NDJSON `{ id, chunk }` envelope). For every appended batch:\n\n1. core calls `append(chunks)` before forwarding the chunks;\n2. the adapter returns exactly one offset per chunk, in order;\n3. core rejects missing, extra, empty, or whitespace/CR/LF-containing offsets;\n4. a resume reads strictly after the supplied offset.\n\nCore never derives an offset from an array index and never stamps one onto the\n`StreamChunk`.\n\n### Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed\n\nA producer that restarts mid-run and replays part of its source must not append\nthe overlap twice. The client has no safety net below its offset de-dup: a\nre-appended chunk carries a new offset, so `seen` never suppresses it, and the\nstream processor concatenates text deltas and tool-call arguments\nunconditionally. That is duplicated prose and `{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}` arguments, not a\ndegraded experience.\n\nThe mechanism for it is `snapshot()` plus plain `append`, not caller-chosen\noffsets. Read what the log already holds, compare it against your replay,\nsuppress the matching prefix, and append only the remainder:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nasync function appendAfterStored(\n  request: Request,\n  replayed: Array<StreamChunk>,\n) {\n  const durability = memoryStream(request)\n  const stored = await durability.snapshot()\n  // `snapshot` returns without waiting, even while the log is open, so this\n  // works on a log whose previous producer died without calling `close()`.\n  const remainder = replayed.slice(stored.length)\n  if (remainder.length > 0) await durability.append(remainder)\n}\n```\n\nThis is what `@tanstack/ai-sandbox` does for a replayed agent run, comparing by\na timestamp-insensitive fingerprint rather than by count, and throwing if the\nreplay and the log disagree inside the prefix. See\n[The Run Journal](../sandbox/journal) for that path in full.\n\nBecause the log stays append-only with adapter-minted offsets, this works on\n`durableStream` too, which is the point: it is the recommended production\nadapter and it has no `upsert`. Two caveats on `durableStream.snapshot()`\nspecifically. It is bounded by an internal ceiling on how many windows it will\npull before giving up on a backend that never reports the reader caught up, and\nit reads from `-1`, so it cannot answer `[]` for a stream the backend never\ncreated; that is a failed call, not an empty run.\n\n`upsert` remains available as an optional capability for adapters whose store\ncan write at a caller-chosen key. Nothing in the resume path above uses it, and\nmost integrations never ask for it. `memoryStream` offers it, `durableStream`\ndoes not (see above). See\n[Re-persisting a stored range](./custom-adapter#re-persisting-a-stored-range) to\nbuild your own.\n\n## Cloudflare Durable Streams\n\n[Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) ships a Cloudflare Workers plus\nDurable Objects backend that speaks this protocol, so `durableStream` talks to\nit directly with no new adapter.\n\nWhen your TanStack AI endpoint also runs on Workers, reach the backend over a\n[service binding](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/)\ninstead of a public URL. The adapter's injectable `fetch` routes every request\nthrough the binding, so traffic stays on Cloudflare's network and the binding\n(not a bearer token) authorizes the call:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\ninterface Env {\n  // A service binding to the deployed Durable Streams Worker.\n  DURABLE_STREAMS: { fetch: typeof fetch }\n}\n\nfunction cloudflareAdapter(request: Request, env: Env) {\n  // No `server` needed: the binding routes by path, so the adapter uses an\n  // internal placeholder base and only the `/streams/...` path matters.\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n    fetch: env.DURABLE_STREAMS.fetch.bind(env.DURABLE_STREAMS),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nIf the backend runs elsewhere, point `server` at the Worker's public URL\ninstead:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\nfunction urlAdapter(request: Request) {\n  return durableStream(request, {\n    server: 'https://durable-streams.example.workers.dev',\n    streamPrefix: 'chat-runs',\n  })\n}\n```\n\nRunning on a Durable Object also satisfies the lease/reaper described under\n[Process death](#process-death): a DO alarm can terminalize a run whose producer\ndied, so a reconnecting client sees a terminal state instead of waiting forever.\n\n## Process death\n\nA process that has already terminated cannot run cleanup code, so literal\nprocess death cannot be guaranteed by `finally` or `close()` alone. Production\nbackends should add a lease/reaper:\n\n1. the producer acquires or renews a lease while writing;\n2. a timer, alarm, or background worker detects expired leases;\n3. the reaper records an aborted terminal state and closes the log;\n4. readers observe that terminal state instead of waiting forever.\n\nThis belongs to the durability service or deployment, not the in-process\nresponse helper.\n\nOne case has a mechanism rather than only advice. A **sandboxed** run's work\nlives on inside the sandbox rather than in the dead process, so a later request\ncan adopt it: `sandboxRunDriver` claims the run, fences out the host that died,\nand resumes appending to the same log. That is a takeover of a live producer, not\nterminalization of a dead one, and it only applies to sandboxed runs — see\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover). The advice above still stands for\neverything else. Note also that `memoryStream` cannot participate: its log lives\nin the producer's own process, so nothing survives that process to be adopted.\n\n## Delivery is not state\n\nThe durability log replays chunks. It is not a queryable source of truth for\nthread messages or conversation history. It answers \"what did this run stream?\",\nnot \"what has this user said?\". Keep authoritative state in your own storage.\nSee [Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence) for the client-side options.\n"}
